WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.540
[upbeat music]

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.460
Let's now look at different options
for packaging chiplet technologies.

3
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.840
The evolution of packaging has
enabled new levels of integration,

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.420
performance, and scalability
in modern semiconductor design.

5
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.020
Chiplet packaging is defined largely on
the first connection between the chip

6
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.500
and the base on which it sits,
the interposer or substrate.

7
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.180
This first connection
affects data transfer speeds,

8
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.160
signal integrity, chip
size, and overall cost.

9
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:40.400
Semiconductor packaging varies from
basic single chip designs to complex

10
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.460
3D stacking with intermediate
solutions using bridges or interposers.

11
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:52.330
First, 2D packaging places chiplets side
by side with bridged interconnects on

12
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.020
organic substrate or a fan-out
redistribution layer.

13
00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.690
Such packaging methods are ideal
for cost-sensitive applications

14
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.960
like mobile and IoT driven by
digital or analog disaggregation.

15
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.840
Higher cost 2.5D and 3D packaging

16
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.560
suits high-performance data
center and automotive compute.

17
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.170
However, these cost models may shift

18
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.600
as technology and industry
capacity evolve.

19
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.690
2.5D packaging builds on
this by introducing a silicon

20
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.000
interposer with through-silicon
vias or TSVs.

21
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:38.080
A TSV is a vertical electrical connection
that passes through a silicon wafer.

22
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.370
It enables high density
interconnects for 2.5D

23
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.240
and 3D packaging of integrated circuits,

24
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.960
allowing for shorter wiring
and improved performance.

25
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:56.440
Next, 2.5D packaging introduces
a passive TSV interposer,

26
00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:02.520
whereas 3D packaging uses an active
TSV interposer and hybrid bonding.

27
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.900
2.5D is like arranging chiplets
on a special connecting platform.

28
00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:11.840
3D is like stacking chiplets
on top of each other.

29
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.540
3D provides the best performance,
but at a higher cost,

30
00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:22.080
while 2.5D offers a good balance
between performance and cost.

31
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:28.070
Finally, in 3D packaging, hybrid
bonding offers the highest interconnect

32
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.000
density by combining dielectric
and metal bonding at the wafer level.

33
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.080
This technique enables
extremely small bump pitches,

34
00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.020
improving signal integrity
and reducing power consumption.

35
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.080
Chiplet packaging technologies
continue to evolve,

36
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.980
driving the next generation of computing.

37
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.560
Let's continue our journey into
the future of chiplet packaging.

38
00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.880
[upbeat music]

